### Let, For, This

In JavaScript, **`let`**, **`for`**, and **`this`** serve very different purposes.

The keyword **`let`** declares a variable whose value can change:

```javascript
let score = 0;
```

A **`for`** loop repeats a block of code, often using a variable declared with `let`:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
```

The keyword **`this`** refers to the object associated with the current execution context. Inside an object method, it usually represents the object itself:

```javascript
const player = {
  name: "Alex",
  introduce() {
    console.log("I am " + this.name);
  }
};
```

Together, these keywords help developers manage data, repeat actions, and work with objects.
